1
00:00:10,130 --> 00:00:28,340
Good morning everyone. Hi, so the semester has started officially today. I'm glad to see you here.

2
00:00:30,020 --> 00:00:33,420
Just a couple of announcements. Also hi to the Extension School students.

3
00:00:33,800 --> 00:00:37,440
Last time we said hi to New Zealand, today we're saying hi to Ireland.

4
00:00:42,660 --> 00:00:52,240
in terms of sectioning for the for the undergrads and graduate students here you will get an email

5
00:00:52,240 --> 00:01:00,120
from sean acor tomorrow and you will put in your section preference we are we are sectioning this

6
00:01:00,120 --> 00:01:06,080
weekend so it will be important that you submit your top preferences and next week we're we're

7
00:01:06,080 --> 00:01:15,250
starting. So last time, if you remember the question that we asked, the guiding question

8
00:01:15,250 --> 00:01:22,330
was why positive psychology? And I mentioned three reasons why we need it as an independent

9
00:01:22,950 --> 00:01:33,690
field as opposed to just being, well, let's do some studies on happiness. Let's do some studies on relationships as it has always been done. The reason why we need a positive psychology is to

10
00:01:33,690 --> 00:01:40,590
shift the pendulum from the 21 to 1 ratio that we have today. For every one study on

11
00:01:40,590 --> 00:01:47,790
depression or anxiety, we have 21 studies on happiness or well-being, we have 21 studies

12
00:01:47,790 --> 00:01:58,790
on depression and anxiety. And we want to shift that pendulum slightly. And I mentioned three reasons why we want to shift this pendulum, despite the fact that there are rising levels

13
00:01:58,790 --> 00:02:05,790
of depression around the world. That anxiety has become an epidemic globally. On college

14
00:02:05,790 --> 00:02:14,330
campuses in the United States, China, Australia, UK. Despite that fact, I argued that we need

15
00:02:14,330 --> 00:02:25,630
to shift that pendulum and do more, quote unquote, positive research. Or in other words, research that focuses on what works. And the reason is, the first reason that we gave, and we're

16
00:02:25,630 --> 00:02:36,650
just finishing up is because the questions that we ask, whether it's the research questions that we ask or the questions that we ask of ourselves or of our partners matter. And if our only questions

17
00:02:36,650 --> 00:02:44,230
or primarily our questions are, what is not working? What is the problem? Why are so many

18
00:02:44,230 --> 00:02:51,270
kids failing? Why are so many kids failing as a result of their circumstances? If we only ask

19
00:02:51,270 --> 00:02:58,100
these questions, we will miss, literally miss an important part of reality. Just like you

20
00:02:58,100 --> 00:03:06,840
miss the children on the bus in the exercise. Most of you did. And if we also ask the positive

21
00:03:06,840 --> 00:03:16,820
question, then suddenly new possibilities, new quests open up. Just like they did for the researchers when they started to ask, no longer why do so many individuals fail,

22
00:03:16,820 --> 00:03:28,480
but started to ask, what do some individuals do and succeed? Why do some individuals succeed despite the unfavorable circumstances?

23
00:03:29,920 --> 00:03:32,220
And then we had the story of Marva Collins,

24
00:03:32,680 --> 00:03:37,500
who exemplifies so many of the themes that we'll talk about throughout the course.

25
00:03:40,340 --> 00:03:45,140
What Marva Collins did was help her students shift from the passive victim,

26
00:03:45,140 --> 00:03:56,600
You're a victim of your circumstances, of your upbringing, neighborhood, country, whatever it is, from a passive victim to an active agent.

27
00:03:56,720 --> 00:03:59,540
Yes, it's difficult, it's tough, it's unfair.

28
00:04:00,120 --> 00:04:02,680
However, it's your responsibility. No one is coming.

29
00:04:03,440 --> 00:04:06,040
It's up to you to make that difference in your lives.

30
00:04:06,540 --> 00:04:18,080
And she made a difference to thousands of people's lives and continues to do so. In other words, if we look at the case of the Chicago school system where Marva Collins was teaching,

31
00:04:18,480 --> 00:04:26,840
the conventional, traditional question was, if you remember, how can we keep these students in school for as long as possible?

32
00:04:27,240 --> 00:04:35,840
How can we keep them in school beyond the age of 10 or 12 so that they don't join street gangs,

33
00:04:36,080 --> 00:04:42,560
so that they are not hurt by drugs or crime?

34
00:04:44,040 --> 00:04:49,060
How can we keep them safe in school? An important question to ask. However, not enough.

35
00:04:49,620 --> 00:04:53,480
Marva Collins comes along and reframes the question.

36
00:04:54,400 --> 00:04:56,260
And her question becomes,

37
00:04:57,240 --> 00:05:00,840
how can we cultivate the seed of greatness in our students?

38
00:05:01,420 --> 00:05:13,080
And that, once again, made all the difference because she saw the seed of greatness in each student. She saw the strength, the virtues in each single student.

39
00:05:14,860 --> 00:05:18,680
a seed, a strength, a virtue, a competence

40
00:05:21,230 --> 00:05:31,590
that other teachers did not see because they did not ask this question because they asked, metaphorically speaking how many geometrical shapes do you see on the screen

41
00:05:32,590 --> 00:05:43,590
and they completely missed the children on the bus they completely missed the seed of greatness and when we don't see the seed of greatness when we don't water it and shed light on it it withers and dies

42
00:05:43,590 --> 00:05:46,990
which is unfortunately the fate of most human potential.

43
00:05:49,370 --> 00:05:55,030
Wherever we go, that is the fate of most human potential, interpersonally in relationships, in most organizations,

44
00:05:55,810 --> 00:05:58,670
in most universities, in most individuals.

45
00:05:59,830 --> 00:06:10,570
Questions make a difference, which is why it is also important to ask the positive psychology question, which is the salutogenic question. What is the source of health?

46
00:06:10,790 --> 00:06:13,650
What is the source of success? What is the source of well-being?

47
00:06:17,420 --> 00:06:21,730
So that's the first reason. The second reason, before I go to the second reason,

48
00:06:22,500 --> 00:06:30,450
if Marva Collins was here today, here is the question that she would be asking us. How can we cultivate the seed of greatness in ourselves and families,

49
00:06:30,750 --> 00:06:40,290
in our communities and organizations, in our nation, and in our world? And when we ask this question, this very important question,

50
00:06:41,010 --> 00:06:44,050
suddenly we see possibilities that we didn't see before.

51
00:06:44,050 --> 00:07:05,130
The second reason for having positive psychology as a field of study and focusing on what works and focusing on researching happiness and relationships and well-being is because happiness does not spontaneously arise when we take unhappiness away.

52
00:07:05,950 --> 00:07:14,350
Now, happiness and unhappiness or happiness and neurosis and psychosis and depression are interconnected, of course.

53
00:07:14,350 --> 00:07:20,930
It's very difficult to be happy if we're experiencing severe depression or anxiety.

54
00:07:23,500 --> 00:07:31,700
So they're certainly interconnected. However, just getting rid of the anxiety or the depression will not in and of itself make us happy,

55
00:07:33,100 --> 00:07:44,140
which is the conventional wisdom today, which is the conventional wisdom of many psychologists, practicing psychologists. Well, let's just get rid of that depression and then everything will be fine. It won't.

56
00:07:44,140 --> 00:07:52,800
And the analogy to explain this is think about the ability to enjoy food, a gourmet meal.

57
00:07:53,900 --> 00:08:01,720
Now, if we have indigestion, it is very difficult to enjoy food. So yes, we first need to get rid of the indigestion.

58
00:08:02,000 --> 00:08:13,560
However, that in and of itself does not guarantee us that we enjoy food. We have to go out and eat the gourmet food to enjoy it. Just getting rid of indigestion is not sufficient.

59
00:08:14,140 --> 00:08:15,320
We need the next step.

60
00:08:16,680 --> 00:08:27,220
Many ways you can look at most of our experiences, psychological affective experiences on a continuum, where some of them fall below the zero, the negative experiences, or the painful experiences,

61
00:08:27,500 --> 00:08:32,500
and the positive or the pleasurable experiences between the zero and the positive.

62
00:08:33,360 --> 00:08:37,720
Neurosis, anger, anxiety, depression, psychosis, to name a few.

63
00:08:38,380 --> 00:08:49,200
On the negative side, the painful side, well-being, satisfaction, joy, excitement, happiness, on the other side, which is the side that positive psychology studies.

64
00:08:49,560 --> 00:08:54,120
Again, getting rid of the negative does not guarantee us the positive,

65
00:08:54,360 --> 00:09:02,160
which is why already in the 1840s, David Henry Thoreau wrote that

66
00:09:02,160 --> 00:09:07,820
most men lead lives of quiet desperation, where it's okay.

67
00:09:08,140 --> 00:09:14,890
There's nothing really wrong, but it's just somewhere there.

68
00:09:14,890 --> 00:09:25,860
In the words of Pink Floyd, people are comfortably numb, comfortably numb, not enough. How can we get beyond that comfortably numb?

69
00:09:26,000 --> 00:09:35,740
How can we get beyond that quiet, quiet, okay, desperation to excitement, to joy, to happiness? And in order to do that, we need to cultivate these traits.

70
00:09:35,980 --> 00:09:41,480
Once again, they don't spontaneously emerge once the painful experiences go away.

71
00:09:41,980 --> 00:09:44,120
And that is why we need a positive psychology.

72
00:09:45,200 --> 00:09:54,900
Positive psychology essentially focuses on the health model, salutogenesis. What is the source of health? Physical, psychological, emotional.

73
00:09:55,400 --> 00:10:04,040
How do we get people to flourish intellectually, emotionally, psychologically, interpersonally, intra-personally?

74
00:10:04,180 --> 00:10:09,600
How do we get them to thrive beyond just getting rid of what's not working in their lives?

75
00:10:10,180 --> 00:10:21,000
And under that model, we see two extremes on many levels. Here at the first level, do we focus on weaknesses, which is what the disease models say,

76
00:10:21,080 --> 00:10:28,420
let's get rid of weaknesses, or do we focus on strength? Well, when you ask people this question, and this was done by the Gallup organization,

77
00:10:28,680 --> 00:10:35,680
a poll, global poll, whether it's in Japan, China, United States, or Europe,

78
00:10:36,220 --> 00:10:41,180
Most people think that it's more important to focus on their weaknesses if they are to succeed.

79
00:10:41,660 --> 00:10:42,520
Big mistake.

80
00:10:43,000 --> 00:10:50,140
The people who focus primarily, not only, remember the also, who shift the pendulum, who focus more on their strength,

81
00:10:50,940 --> 00:10:54,000
are not only happier, they're also in the long run more successful.

82
00:10:54,880 --> 00:10:57,180
It applies to leadership as well.

83
00:10:58,260 --> 00:11:02,860
Positive psychology says let's focus also on our strength, at least as much.

84
00:11:04,180 --> 00:11:13,860
in an organization, as well as on the individual level? Are we focused mostly on overcoming deficiencies or building the competencies, what we're good at and getting better at it?

85
00:11:14,500 --> 00:11:19,740
What our natural inclination, individually or organizationally, do we focus on that and then

86
00:11:19,740 --> 00:11:27,200
build on that? Again, tied to success as well as well-being, if we're more toward the positive

87
00:11:27,200 --> 00:11:37,030
side. How do we live our lives? Running away from painful experiences or actively seeking

88
00:11:37,030 --> 00:11:48,250
pleasure? Running away from unhappiness or adhering to the declaration and pursuing happiness?

89
00:11:49,130 --> 00:11:59,210
Now, that may look quite similar. For example, someone may be working 80 hours a week, running away from something, running away from issues at home, running away from dealing

90
00:11:59,210 --> 00:12:10,670
with intrapersonal issues, and that may look exactly the same as a person who works 80 hours a week and who is extremely passionate about what she does.

91
00:12:12,700 --> 00:12:15,940
May look the same, but from the inside, they feel very different.

92
00:12:17,680 --> 00:12:25,320
One is the disease model. Let's run away from what's not working. The other is the health model. Let's pursue my passions, what I love to do.

93
00:12:27,540 --> 00:12:35,340
With a disease model, the optimum level is the zero. Let's just be okay. Let's just not hurt.

94
00:12:36,760 --> 00:12:44,580
And again, that's important to get rid of hurt. It's important to get rid of depression. But what the health model is saying, that's not enough. Let's go beyond that.

95
00:12:45,140 --> 00:12:47,860
Let's go to the excitement, to the fun.

96
00:12:49,850 --> 00:12:57,830
Because the ideal is not just a tensionless state. It is the creative tension. And we'll talk about it and we'll read about it when we do flow.

97
00:12:58,270 --> 00:13:02,410
Flow is the state where we're excited, where we're engaged in what we're doing.

98
00:13:03,810 --> 00:13:07,010
Where it's much more than being comfortably numb.

99
00:13:07,430 --> 00:13:13,250
In fact, it's being a little bit uncomfortable. It's being outside of our comfort zone. It's being in our stretch zone.

100
00:13:13,970 --> 00:13:17,210
Not the panic zone where it hurts, the stretch zone.

101
00:13:18,170 --> 00:13:22,330
Where there is excitement, where there is some nervousness, there is also growth there.

102
00:13:26,760 --> 00:13:27,780
So what do you want?

103
00:13:29,040 --> 00:13:40,240
Where do you want to go? What do you want to pursue? Do you want to run away from pleasure and run away from pain? Do you want to run away from unhappiness or do you want to pursue happiness and pleasure?

104
00:13:41,540 --> 00:13:47,640
Do you want to focus primarily on your deficiencies, your weaknesses or your strengths?

105
00:13:48,740 --> 00:13:50,800
What is the optimum? What is the ideal?

106
00:13:52,480 --> 00:14:00,060
Is there a glass ceiling, the zero, or can it go on and on, more excitement, more enjoyment?

107
00:14:01,460 --> 00:14:09,920
more passion. Now, there's something threatening about the health model, because there is really

108
00:14:09,920 --> 00:14:15,100
no limits, and there are less prescriptions there, certainly today, than in the disease model.

109
00:14:16,100 --> 00:14:23,660
Positive psychology, the field of health psychology, is in its infancy. There's much

110
00:14:23,660 --> 00:14:29,460
more research, much more advice out there on how to get rid of depression than on how do I pursue

111
00:14:29,460 --> 00:14:38,220
my strength. But fortunately, and again, this is why positive psychology as a network of scholars

112
00:14:38,220 --> 00:14:49,620
applying themselves to these ideas and ideals is so important. Because today, as you'll see throughout the semester, there are so many more tools that we can apply to our lives to go beyond

113
00:14:50,580 --> 00:15:02,380
the zero. That's not all. So we said that there are three reasons. It's about where we focus.

114
00:15:02,380 --> 00:15:13,180
focus creates reality. It's about happiness. It's not just the negation of unhappiness. The third reason why positive psychology is important is because positive psychology and

115
00:15:13,180 --> 00:15:18,280
the areas that we study and more importantly apply within the field of positive psychology

116
00:15:18,280 --> 00:15:27,420
do not just take us from the zero to the positive. They also help us deal with the negative.

117
00:15:27,420 --> 00:15:33,840
They help us deal with anxiety and depression and painful experiences and emotions.

118
00:15:38,960 --> 00:15:44,460
When we cultivate the positive, we're essentially focusing on prevention.

119
00:15:46,400 --> 00:15:47,440
Let me explain.

120
00:15:54,240 --> 00:16:05,820
What has been found over the last 10 years and a little bit longer is that the most effective way of actually dealing with the rising levels of depression in our culture, with individual depression or anxiety

121
00:16:05,820 --> 00:16:10,420
is actually not to focus on the depression and anxiety directly.

122
00:16:11,220 --> 00:16:13,440
That is important as well.

123
00:16:14,640 --> 00:16:22,660
It was found that the most effective way of dealing with this phenomenon was actually to cultivate the positive, to cultivate personal strength,

124
00:16:23,020 --> 00:16:27,340
to cultivate and identify one's passions,

125
00:16:28,740 --> 00:16:35,320
to ask a question such as, what is meaningful to me in my life? What's my purpose? Why am I here?

126
00:16:36,060 --> 00:16:40,540
What do I really, really want to do once I graduate?

127
00:16:40,980 --> 00:16:44,360
People who ask these questions and spend time on these questions

128
00:16:45,780 --> 00:16:50,660
are much more likely to begin a quest that is not devoid of the painful emotions.

129
00:16:51,240 --> 00:16:52,560
No quest is.

130
00:16:53,240 --> 00:16:58,020
but a quest that is more enjoyable, more pleasurable, more meaningful

131
00:16:59,640 --> 00:17:02,920
and also more successful as it turns out.

132
00:17:03,320 --> 00:17:10,000
But more importantly, more successful in what I call the ultimate currency which I think is the currency of happiness and well-being.

133
00:17:11,240 --> 00:17:17,640
The reason is because there are two different approaches to dealing with illness.

134
00:17:18,300 --> 00:17:24,760
One, the positive psychological approach is that the illness is the absence of health,

135
00:17:27,920 --> 00:17:31,560
as opposed to health is the absence of illness. I mean, think about the disease model.

136
00:17:31,700 --> 00:17:40,420
The disease model is we're sick because we're ill.

137
00:17:42,680 --> 00:17:48,400
Did you listen to that? We're sick because we're ill. That was very profound. You should think about it for a while here.

138
00:17:49,080 --> 00:17:51,760
I'll add a time just to marinate a little bit so that you can.

139
00:17:52,520 --> 00:17:53,800
Let me start that again.

140
00:17:57,820 --> 00:18:06,660
If we take away the illness, then we become healthy. That's the model, the conventional model. Take away the illness, you become healthy, it's good.

141
00:18:08,240 --> 00:18:11,420
The positive psychological model is slightly different.

142
00:18:12,860 --> 00:18:23,920
It is you're ill because you don't have enough health in your life. Because you're not pursuing those things that make you healthy.

143
00:18:24,140 --> 00:18:25,260
And what makes you healthy?

144
00:18:25,760 --> 00:18:28,700
The things that I spoke about before, pursuing meaning.

145
00:18:29,060 --> 00:18:36,360
Purpose, cultivating healthy relationships. And when we don't have these things, that's when illness comes in.

146
00:18:36,860 --> 00:18:44,240
Now the difference between the two models, the health model and the disease model, is more than just semantic.

147
00:18:46,000 --> 00:18:51,720
Here is Abraham Maslow talking about neurosis.

148
00:18:55,170 --> 00:19:02,710
Neurosis is a falling short of what one could have been, and even, one could say, of what one should have been.

149
00:19:03,350 --> 00:19:08,270
Biologically speaking, that is, if one had grown and developed in an unimpeded way.

150
00:19:09,130 --> 00:19:11,990
Human and personal possibilities have been lost.

151
00:19:12,350 --> 00:19:15,230
The world has been narrowed, and so has consciousness.

152
00:19:16,070 --> 00:19:18,070
Capacities have been inhibited.

153
00:19:18,530 --> 00:19:30,030
Let me explain what he means here. What he means is that we're ill because we do not cultivate what we're about enough. We do not become self-actualized.

154
00:19:30,150 --> 00:19:35,830
We don't do what we're supposed to do. We diminish ourselves, and that's when we become ill.

155
00:19:36,190 --> 00:19:37,490
That's when we're unwell.

156
00:19:38,230 --> 00:19:43,770
This is very different from the disease model that says, okay, you're unwell, deal with that illness.

157
00:19:44,950 --> 00:19:48,370
What he's saying, no, you're unwell, focus on your health.

158
00:19:48,810 --> 00:19:49,790
Strengthen your health.

159
00:19:50,310 --> 00:20:02,200
Because you're ill because you haven't focused enough on your health. He calls that, and I quote, a failure of personal growth.

160
00:20:02,520 --> 00:20:13,740
That's when we experience neurosis. When we don't cultivate ourselves enough, when we don't cultivate our relationships enough, that's when we fail. And what positive psychology is all about is precisely that.

161
00:20:14,260 --> 00:20:22,680
Cultivating personal growth. Working on the positive. And when we work on the positive, on what comes on this side of the graph that you saw up there,

162
00:20:23,060 --> 00:20:30,580
On the positive side, when we cultivate these things, it also helps us deal much better with the negative when that arises.

163
00:20:31,140 --> 00:20:38,580
I want to quote Marty Seligman, who talks about precisely this idea.

164
00:20:39,420 --> 00:20:43,400
In the last decade, psychologists have become concerned with prevention.

165
00:20:44,430 --> 00:20:54,820
How can we prevent problems like depression or substance abuse or schizophrenia in young people who are genetically vulnerable or who live in worlds that nurture these problems?

166
00:20:55,400 --> 00:21:03,320
How can we prevent murderous schoolyard violence in children who have access to weapons, poor parental supervision, and a mean streak?

167
00:21:04,420 --> 00:21:13,940
Now he's asking this question, and the disease model response to this is we need to help them deal directly with their depression and with their anxiety

168
00:21:13,940 --> 00:21:18,960
and with their unhappiness so that we can prevent all these social ills.

169
00:21:20,100 --> 00:21:23,220
Whether it's violence, whether it's unhappiness.

170
00:21:23,820 --> 00:21:26,780
What he's saying here is the following.

171
00:21:27,000 --> 00:21:36,080
What we have learned over 50 years is that the disease model does not move us closer to the prevention of these serious problems.

172
00:21:37,100 --> 00:21:48,260
Indeed, the major strides in prevention have largely come from a perspective focused on systematically building competency, not correcting weakness.

173
00:21:49,120 --> 00:21:50,980
In other words, the health model.

174
00:21:51,860 --> 00:21:58,940
Let's work on competencies. Let's work on strength. Let's work on relationships. Let's help them identify something meaningful in their life, their passion.

175
00:21:59,520 --> 00:22:04,700
And that's how we will, over time, also help what comes on the negative side.

176
00:22:06,160 --> 00:22:10,060
Health model versus the disease model that goes directly to deal with the disease.

177
00:22:11,600 --> 00:22:16,180
Now again, Seligman is not saying to the exclusion of. He's saying also.

178
00:22:19,320 --> 00:22:20,280
He continues.

179
00:22:21,620 --> 00:22:40,400
We have discovered that there are human strengths that act as buffers against mental illness, courage, future-mindedness, optimism, interpersonal skill, faith, work ethic, hope, honesty, perseverance, the capacity for flow and insight, to name several.

180
00:22:40,720 --> 00:22:49,440
We've shown that learning optimism prevents depression and anxiety in children and adults, roughly halving their incidence over the next few years.

181
00:22:50,180 --> 00:22:59,620
Similarly, I believe that if we wish to prevent drug abuse in teenagers who grow up in a neighborhood that puts them at risk, that the effect of prevention is not remedial.

182
00:23:00,160 --> 00:23:08,060
Rather, it consists of identifying and amplifying the strength that these teens already have.

183
00:23:09,540 --> 00:23:11,360
It's exactly what Marva Collins did.

184
00:23:12,040 --> 00:23:14,700
Focus on the health and cultivated that.

185
00:23:15,520 --> 00:23:18,860
Watered it and shed light on it and realized it.

186
00:23:20,720 --> 00:23:24,580
We'll talk about all these ideas throughout the course.

187
00:23:27,080 --> 00:23:37,900
What the health model does, this is a theme that will go throughout the course, is cultivate capacity. It cultivates the capacity to deal with the negatives when these arise,

188
00:23:38,060 --> 00:23:42,340
whether it's negatives and painful experiences in relationships or in ourselves.

189
00:23:43,200 --> 00:23:47,040
What does that mean? What does that mean? Well, let me draw on two analogies.

190
00:23:47,740 --> 00:23:55,540
Cultivating capacities is about creating a strong psychological immune system. These are the words of Nathaniel Brandon.

191
00:23:56,520 --> 00:24:03,220
Psychological immune system. What happens when we have a strong physical immune system? Does it mean that we don't get ill? Of course not. We do.

192
00:24:03,360 --> 00:24:09,840
But it means that we get ill less often, and when we do get sick, we recover more promptly.

193
00:24:09,840 --> 00:24:20,040
This is exactly what cultivating strength, optimism, sense of purpose, meaning, mindfulness.

194
00:24:20,500 --> 00:24:29,840
This is exactly what these characteristics do. They enlarge, transform the way we see, the way we experience the world.

195
00:24:30,380 --> 00:24:38,580
Enlarge the capacity of the form. And thus we're better able to deal with inevitable difficulties. And there are inevitable difficulties.

196
00:24:40,160 --> 00:24:43,040
no life is completely immune to those.

197
00:24:46,240 --> 00:24:51,580
So it strengthens our immune system. Another analogy that we can use is of an engine.

198
00:24:52,860 --> 00:25:02,800
If we have a small engine and we have to pull a car up a steep hill, a difficult hill, the engine is more likely to collapse, to blow up.

199
00:25:02,880 --> 00:25:08,920
Whereas if our engine is large, we're much more likely to get up that hill

200
00:25:09,880 --> 00:25:14,800
and to do it more gracefully with relative ease.

201
00:25:15,700 --> 00:25:18,040
So what we're doing when we cultivate the positive,

202
00:25:20,010 --> 00:25:28,170
we're strengthening our, metaphorically speaking, psychological engine, and we're better able to deal with the negative to zero.

203
00:25:29,410 --> 00:25:33,190
Not to mention the fact that we're also able to become happier.

204
00:25:33,890 --> 00:25:45,180
Because happiness does not just come spontaneously when we negate unhappiness. I want to go back to our local village.

205
00:25:45,540 --> 00:25:51,200
So remember last time I put up the article by the Crimson?

206
00:25:51,900 --> 00:25:59,080
That was, unfortunately, I couldn't find a more recent one. That was in 2004. But the situation is rather similar today.

207
00:25:59,280 --> 00:26:06,700
And one of the things that the Crimson article says is that we need to put more resources into mental health at Harvard.

208
00:26:08,360 --> 00:26:12,660
And that's important. I agree. However, that's only part of the picture.

209
00:26:13,880 --> 00:26:22,100
What we also need to do is put the resources in some different places as well, not only as well.

210
00:26:22,780 --> 00:26:31,460
Because it's not just enough to put the resources in places that help us deal with our depression and anxiety and unhappiness directly.

211
00:26:32,300 --> 00:26:38,520
It is also important to put these resources in places that help us cultivate capacity.

212
00:26:38,520 --> 00:26:44,380
the capacity to deal with these difficulties and hardships when they will arise, and they will arise.

213
00:26:45,320 --> 00:26:53,320
In other words, there has to be more resources put in place, such as helping students identify their passions when they come here,

214
00:26:53,660 --> 00:26:57,660
helping students identify their sense of meaning in life,

215
00:26:59,180 --> 00:27:10,920
helping students identify what they really, really, really want to do, Helping students overcome the pull, the external pull that is often there,

216
00:27:11,400 --> 00:27:13,740
taking them away from their core.

217
00:27:14,160 --> 00:27:18,780
Helping them chip away those limitations, those voices.

218
00:27:19,400 --> 00:27:22,900
Helping them identify who they really are.

219
00:27:24,000 --> 00:27:26,200
Helping them identify their strengths.

220
00:27:27,160 --> 00:27:29,900
And then pursuing those while here at Harvard.

221
00:27:30,760 --> 00:27:39,860
All these capabilities, all these skills are skills that are mostly, and I'm not just talking about Harvard, globally are not taught in schools.

222
00:27:41,460 --> 00:27:42,820
And we need to teach them.

223
00:27:44,040 --> 00:27:55,320
This is not to say that what is going on at Harvard with the numerous resources that we do have here is not important. It is very important. Just a small example, the Bureau of Study Council.

224
00:27:55,560 --> 00:28:05,260
I don't know how many of you have used that resource. I've used it as an undergrad. I still use it, now I'm doing some work with them, did some work with them last year, and they're wonderful.

225
00:28:07,200 --> 00:28:17,540
And at the same time, we also need to cultivate the positive, to think more of the zero to the positive side of the equation as well.

226
00:28:18,100 --> 00:28:25,080
And this is what positive psychology does, and I hope this is what 1504, to some extent, will help do.

227
00:28:25,080 --> 00:28:36,390
To summarize, the message of the positive psychology movement is to remind our field that it has been deformed.

228
00:28:37,170 --> 00:28:44,210
Psychology is not just the study of disease, weakness, and damage. It also is the study of strength and virtue.

229
00:28:44,950 --> 00:28:49,750
Treatment is not just fixing what is wrong. It is also building what is right.

230
00:28:50,370 --> 00:28:58,110
Psychology is not just about illness or health. It is about work, education, insight, love, growth, and play.

231
00:28:58,530 --> 00:29:07,110
And in this quest for what is best, positive psychology does not rely on wishful thinking, self-deception, or hand-waving.

232
00:29:07,370 --> 00:29:16,230
Instead, it tries to adapt what is best in the scientific method to the unique problems that human behavior presents in all its complexity.

233
00:29:16,970 --> 00:29:27,830
It's about bridging the ivory tower and main street in the area, which I believe is the most important one, and talks to each and every one of us.

234
00:29:27,830 --> 00:30:08,570
I want to move on now. I want to move on to the next set of lectures, two or two and a half lectures, where I'm going to be talking about the basic premises of this course.

235
00:30:09,510 --> 00:30:13,330
As I said earlier, this course is not a survey course of positive psychology.

236
00:30:14,030 --> 00:30:17,010
It's very selective. It's about the question of questions.

237
00:30:17,610 --> 00:30:19,910
What can help us as individuals?

238
00:30:20,330 --> 00:30:26,370
What can help our community become happier? Not happy, happier.

239
00:30:27,150 --> 00:30:36,850
So that by the end of the three-month semester, you're happier than you were before. A year from now, you will be hopefully happier than you were at the end of the semester, and so on and so on.

240
00:30:39,070 --> 00:30:49,070
So what are the basic premises? Where am I coming from when I think about these courses? And what I want to do is share with you the five basic premises.

241
00:30:49,330 --> 00:30:54,870
And these premises are going to be presented as something and essentially it's opposite.

242
00:30:56,550 --> 00:31:07,490
So that we're clear from the outset where we're coming from, where I'm coming from, where the teaching staff is coming from. And also so that we can build the foundation of the course.

243
00:31:07,490 --> 00:31:17,510
Remember, I talked, those of you who were here the first time, this course is built like a spiral. Everything is interconnected. What I talked about the first class is connected to what I'm going to talk about today.

244
00:31:17,590 --> 00:31:19,870
It's going to be connected to lecture 19.

245
00:31:21,710 --> 00:31:32,110
So in many ways, the premises coupled with what we discussed in the first two lectures build the foundation of that spiral upon which everything else will be built.

246
00:31:32,450 --> 00:31:34,410
So here are the five basic premises.

247
00:31:35,790 --> 00:31:40,930
I'll go through them briefly now and then elaborate on each in the next couple of lectures,

248
00:31:41,470 --> 00:31:45,150
interweaving them with studies, research, as well as applications.

249
00:31:46,990 --> 00:31:57,170
First, this course, as I mentioned first, is about bridge building. Bridge building among disciplines, it's eclectic, and bridge building in terms of academia and Main Street.

250
00:31:57,730 --> 00:32:05,970
rather than the division and the separation, the specialization that very often is dominant in academia.

251
00:32:06,330 --> 00:32:11,710
This approach of this course, with all its challenges, is the opposite.

252
00:32:13,740 --> 00:32:23,960
Once again, I wouldn't be teaching the class if I didn't think change is possible, although there is a lot of research in psychology, a lot of evidence that shows how difficult change is. So I will argue that change is possible,

253
00:32:24,100 --> 00:32:34,740
whether it's individual change, organizational change, and we'll start to look at how it's possible. Just at the very basic level of the spiral, we have an entire week just devoted to change,

254
00:32:34,920 --> 00:32:36,380
where we'll elaborate on that.

255
00:32:37,400 --> 00:32:39,120
Techniques, methods, tools.

256
00:32:41,700 --> 00:32:52,120
Third premise related to the first, internal factors primarily determine happiness. This is what I'm going to argue for, as opposed to happiness is primarily a function of external circumstances.

257
00:32:53,280 --> 00:33:01,300
Not saying that external circumstances are not important, that we shouldn't focus also on improving them, bettering them, whether for ourselves,

258
00:33:01,340 --> 00:33:12,560
for society at large. However, happiness primarily, not only primarily, is dependent on how we perceive the world, on the form, on our interpretation, on our perception.

259
00:33:15,160 --> 00:33:21,700
Human nature must be obeyed versus human nature must be perfected. This, in many ways,

260
00:33:22,100 --> 00:33:27,700
captures a conflict that has gone through throughout human history, whether you look

261
00:33:27,700 --> 00:33:35,180
at politics, whether you look at religion, whether you look at philosophy, as well as today's psychology.

262
00:33:35,780 --> 00:33:37,500
How do we look at human nature?

263
00:33:38,100 --> 00:33:47,920
Is human nature flawed and therefore needs to be perfected? Or is human nature flawed? Maybe there is something that we don't like about it, but we need to accept it and work with it.

264
00:33:48,620 --> 00:33:59,340
I'm going to argue for the letter of what I just said for the fact that human nature needs to be obeyed with all its flaws, with all its shortcomings,

265
00:33:59,640 --> 00:34:03,420
as opposed to attempting to perfect it on a psychological level.

266
00:34:05,330 --> 00:34:06,970
We'll get to that next time.

267
00:34:08,210 --> 00:34:14,010
Controversial, the very important foundation of mental health and well-being.

268
00:34:14,850 --> 00:34:26,520
And finally, what I'm going to argue for is that happiness is and ought to be the ultimate end, the end which we pursue,

269
00:34:28,690 --> 00:34:39,010
and that is also a moral claim, as opposed to happiness just being another secondary pursuit and that there are pursuits that are higher, more important, more moral than that.

270
00:34:39,530 --> 00:34:44,830
Once again, it may sound controversial, I'll try and reconcile the dis-ease,

271
00:34:44,950 --> 00:34:52,840
the unhappiness that you may experience thinking about that.

272
00:34:53,260 --> 00:34:56,740
Again, more on that next time. So let me begin with bridge building.

273
00:34:58,960 --> 00:35:05,740
Here I'm going back to what I talked about right at the very beginning of the first lecture, the idea of bridging ivory tower and main street.

274
00:35:06,000 --> 00:35:12,300
There are many people in academia, outside of academia, who divide the world essentially into two.

275
00:35:12,540 --> 00:35:20,320
They talk about the real world that is outside, that is dirty, impure, profane,

276
00:35:21,360 --> 00:35:28,880
versus academia, which is lofty, idealistic, sacred.

277
00:35:31,090 --> 00:35:33,050
Sacred versus the profane.

278
00:35:33,730 --> 00:35:41,850
This distinction hurts. It hurts academia and hurts people who are outside of academia.

279
00:35:42,670 --> 00:35:52,790
Alfred North Whitehead, the philosopher, the careful shielding of a university from the activities of the world around us is the best way to chill interest and to defeat progress.

280
00:35:53,350 --> 00:35:58,310
Celibacy does not suit a university. It must mate itself with action.

281
00:36:00,610 --> 00:36:03,850
This is very important for a university.

282
00:36:07,020 --> 00:36:17,720
Sheldon White, who was my thesis advisor as an undergraduate here, talked about a second psychology. And he said a second psychology is a psychology that leaves the labs,

283
00:36:19,000 --> 00:36:28,920
that draws on research done in the labs, that is important and meaningful, however, doesn't only focus on that. It goes out to the outside world,

284
00:36:29,120 --> 00:36:32,540
interacts, gets its hands, its minds dirty,

285
00:36:33,760 --> 00:36:44,560
does work outside and then applies its work and then learns from the quote-unquote dirty experiences and brings it back to the lab and so on and so on in an upward spiral.

286
00:36:44,720 --> 00:36:47,360
He called it the importance of a second psychology,

287
00:36:48,940 --> 00:36:52,920
which is what Alfred North Whitehead is also talking about.

288
00:36:55,200 --> 00:37:02,660
Now you may be sitting here and most of you are not going to go into academia

289
00:37:03,220 --> 00:37:08,800
and you're thinking to yourself, okay, fine, so university, academia must mate itself with action.

290
00:37:11,820 --> 00:37:13,240
What does it have to do with me?

291
00:37:13,860 --> 00:37:15,380
How is this relevant to me?

292
00:37:17,150 --> 00:37:21,550
Not only is it relevant to you, it has everything to do with you.

293
00:37:22,130 --> 00:37:22,930
And here is why.

294
00:37:24,450 --> 00:37:31,590
What the world needs more than anything else is practical idealists.

295
00:37:36,660 --> 00:37:44,780
For six years, I was a resident tutor in Leverett House when I was a graduate student and then also when I started to teach.

296
00:37:45,860 --> 00:37:53,140
And what struck me most about conversations that I had either in Leverett, in other houses,

297
00:37:53,140 --> 00:38:00,260
with students is their sense of mission, your sense of mission,

298
00:38:00,600 --> 00:38:05,200
your desire to do good, to make the world a better place.

299
00:38:06,240 --> 00:38:14,780
And as I followed many of the students after they graduated, whether students who were with me here as undergrads or students whom I tutored,

300
00:38:15,080 --> 00:38:22,520
when I followed the paths, it wasn't just empty words. These students went ahead and did wonderful things.

301
00:38:22,520 --> 00:38:27,340
whether it was right out of college, whether it was once they established themselves.

302
00:38:27,820 --> 00:38:36,220
But there was always this in the back of their mind, very often the fore of their minds. How can I make this world a better place?

303
00:38:37,640 --> 00:38:42,620
Passionate, idealistic, good, in the deepest sense, good.

304
00:38:44,410 --> 00:38:50,210
This desire to make a difference, common to just about all students.

305
00:38:59,800 --> 00:39:06,200
There are many people who talk about this generation as the me generation.

306
00:39:06,520 --> 00:39:14,440
All I care about, all this generation cares about is, well, let me just make more money. Let me just get a bigger house.

307
00:39:14,780 --> 00:39:19,120
Let me just be more successful and accrue more accolades, more prestige.

308
00:39:21,810 --> 00:39:23,370
This is a false stereotype.

309
00:39:24,090 --> 00:39:28,950
Yes, accolades, prestige, money is important. It's important to most people in the world.

310
00:39:29,350 --> 00:39:29,790
Big deal.

311
00:39:30,390 --> 00:39:39,910
But what these people who have these stereotypes, where they are, is that they see just that. And they don't see the desire to make a difference.

312
00:39:40,110 --> 00:39:47,030
You know that 1,800 students at Harvard each year, above 1,800 students, are members of PBHA.

313
00:39:49,100 --> 00:39:53,480
That's not all. There are other students who are outside PBHA and who volunteer.

314
00:39:54,800 --> 00:40:00,380
Just about every single one of you, at least if we look at statistics, just about every single one of you,

315
00:40:01,200 --> 00:40:13,100
Very soon, after you leave Harvard, will join an organization, whether it could be your primary job or not, an organization that is a social enterprise, a not-for-profit, something to better the world.

316
00:40:13,200 --> 00:40:19,800
You'll be on boards of such organizations. You'll donate money to such organizations.

317
00:40:22,070 --> 00:40:27,030
Harvard grads are generous with their time, with their money, with their efforts,

318
00:40:30,700 --> 00:40:38,220
Whether they're at the business school, the law school, college, med school, ed school,

319
00:40:42,050 --> 00:40:44,310
you give a lot because you care.

320
00:40:47,170 --> 00:40:51,670
Again, whether it's money, whether it's time, usually both.

321
00:40:52,870 --> 00:40:54,170
False stereotypes.

322
00:40:54,710 --> 00:40:57,830
There's also false stereotypes about Americans.

323
00:41:00,670 --> 00:41:06,410
Americans, empirically speaking, just like speaking empirically about Harvard students and looking at the trends and the statistics.

324
00:41:06,770 --> 00:41:10,630
Empirically speaking, Americans are the most generous people in the world.

325
00:41:13,120 --> 00:41:15,900
Not just because they have more money to give.

326
00:41:17,200 --> 00:41:20,480
Yes, Americans have more money to give and they give a lot more money.

327
00:41:20,860 --> 00:41:23,320
Whether it's in food, whether it's in medical aid.

328
00:41:25,880 --> 00:41:29,280
Americans also spend the most time, and this is research done at Dartmouth.

329
00:41:29,760 --> 00:41:33,680
Americans spend the most time out of any other people in the world volunteering.

330
00:41:34,160 --> 00:41:36,020
An average of four hours a week.

331
00:41:37,780 --> 00:41:43,440
volunteering outside of their job, which may also have a social objective

332
00:41:44,240 --> 00:41:46,060
more than any other people in the world.

333
00:41:46,460 --> 00:41:50,600
Once again, false stereotypes about this wonderful country.

334
00:41:52,840 --> 00:41:53,960
And this is wonderful.

335
00:41:54,320 --> 00:41:57,020
This is wonderful to see, wonderful to be here,

336
00:41:57,580 --> 00:42:00,460
whether it's at Harvard, whether it's in America, a real privilege.

337
00:42:06,610 --> 00:42:16,770
You see, many of you, not far from now, not a long time, you will be in influential positions where you will be able to do a lot of good in a for-profit organization,

338
00:42:16,950 --> 00:42:20,690
not-for-profit organization on the board of your previous school,

339
00:42:22,430 --> 00:42:24,030
with your money, with your time.

340
00:42:26,490 --> 00:42:28,710
However, and here is the however.

341
00:42:30,590 --> 00:42:35,870
I've met many grads who were students with me or students when I was a tutor

342
00:42:36,390 --> 00:42:39,110
who expressed their frustration to me.

343
00:42:39,910 --> 00:42:48,190
said to me you know I had all the good intentions in the world I have all the good intentions in the world I want to do good I've donated my time my money

344
00:42:49,330 --> 00:42:56,890
but I feel that something is missing I feel that I've fallen short of my potential to really make a difference

345
00:42:58,150 --> 00:43:02,350
why? because goodwill and idealism while necessary

346
00:43:03,570 --> 00:43:04,790
they're not sufficient

347
00:43:06,030 --> 00:43:06,970
not enough

348
00:43:07,890 --> 00:43:15,970
Because very often, with very good intentions, we may fall short of what we're capable of doing

349
00:43:15,970 --> 00:43:20,890
or, in some situations, even hurt more than help.

350
00:43:21,710 --> 00:43:25,490
And we'll look at some of these studies, hopefully still today,

351
00:43:26,150 --> 00:43:28,690
where very good intentions actually hurt more than they helped.

352
00:43:29,270 --> 00:43:34,830
Psychologists for decades had very good intentions about helping at-risk population.

353
00:43:34,830 --> 00:43:42,670
A lot of money, millions and millions of dollars went into that, with very little effect.

354
00:43:43,950 --> 00:43:52,510
Why? Because they didn't also ask a question that they needed to ask, which was, in that particular case, the salutogenic question.

355
00:43:52,850 --> 00:43:57,290
Why do some individuals succeed despite unfavorable circumstances?

356
00:43:57,650 --> 00:44:03,950
There were very good intentions before this question was asked. There was a lot of idealism. It wasn't enough.

357
00:44:03,950 --> 00:44:22,980
And very often, some of these interventions engendered the passive victim mentality as opposed to the active agent mentality that Marva Collins instilled, that programs by Karen Ravitch and Marty Seligman instill.

358
00:44:26,310 --> 00:44:28,410
And this is where psychology can help.

359
00:44:29,770 --> 00:44:36,710
You see, because we can take the research and apply it.

360
00:44:37,270 --> 00:44:47,170
We can take this research and apply it, which goes back to the point why it's so important to bridge Ivory Tower and Main Street. Most of this research is not applied.

361
00:44:47,430 --> 00:44:55,570
So, for example, how many teachers working in the classroom day in and day out know about the Pygmalion effect?

362
00:44:56,410 --> 00:45:01,130
The Pygmalion effect that we're going to talk about next week or the week after

363
00:45:01,850 --> 00:45:12,590
shows how teachers' expectations are self-fulfilling prophecies. And if we have high expectations, if we see the seed of greatness in the student,

364
00:45:13,090 --> 00:45:20,130
that seed of greatness is much more likely to flourish. Whereas if we don't appreciate it, it will depreciate, wither and die.

365
00:45:20,130 --> 00:45:30,110
No many teachers know about these studies and how they create, through their beliefs, a self-fulfilling prophecy when it comes to their students.

366
00:45:31,860 --> 00:45:33,940
How many teachers know about Marville Collins?

367
00:45:34,460 --> 00:45:40,500
Every teacher in the world, on the first day of teacher training, needs to learn about Marville Collins and the Pygmalion effect.

368
00:45:41,000 --> 00:45:42,160
They don't do that.

369
00:45:43,680 --> 00:45:44,900
How about this?

370
00:45:45,560 --> 00:45:52,640
Self-esteem. How do you increase self-esteem? If I had to do a poll here, most people, guaranteed, would say, praise people.

371
00:45:53,640 --> 00:45:56,980
Praise people, praise children, it will enhance their self-esteem.

372
00:45:58,520 --> 00:46:04,360
Right, partially right. And if it's taken as the whole truth and nothing but the truth, it's detrimental.

373
00:46:05,280 --> 00:46:09,320
Because there's a lot of research that shows that when we praise people indiscriminately,

374
00:46:11,100 --> 00:46:16,220
we're actually, in the long run, potentially hurting them more than we're helping them.

375
00:46:17,780 --> 00:46:19,360
whether it's their well-being

376
00:46:20,400 --> 00:46:30,340
as well as their success but the self-esteem movement mostly says praise people praise children all the time and again that's important

377
00:46:30,340 --> 00:46:35,140
but it's also important to know how to praise how many people are familiar with the work

378
00:46:36,140 --> 00:46:39,620
of the Stanford psychologist Carol Dweck

379
00:46:40,640 --> 00:46:45,600
well you're going to be familiar with it in a few weeks

380
00:46:47,850 --> 00:46:57,950
Idealists, many idealists with good intentions are not. And they continue to support or to practice self-esteem as indiscriminate praise,

381
00:46:58,370 --> 00:47:00,390
ultimately hurting more than helping.

382
00:47:00,950 --> 00:47:11,650
How many psychologists or rather how many interventionists with good intentions are familiar with the work of Albert Bendura on self-efficacy and how you cultivate that?

383
00:47:11,990 --> 00:47:13,070
Not enough.

384
00:47:13,770 --> 00:47:16,590
And very often more harm than good is created.

385
00:47:20,020 --> 00:47:25,060
How many psychologists are familiar with this new emerging field of the mind and body?

386
00:47:25,940 --> 00:47:45,760
How many know about the studies that show, for example, that yoga practice diminishes significantly, significantly, more than any other intervention that they tried, the likelihood of second-time offenders when they practice it in jail?

387
00:47:45,760 --> 00:47:51,920
after they are released, they are much less likely to return to jail if they did some yoga there.

388
00:47:56,040 --> 00:47:57,120
Strange, but true.

389
00:47:58,280 --> 00:48:01,720
How many people know that, and this is connected to my first point,

390
00:48:02,540 --> 00:48:07,780
that doing meditation actually literally transforms our brain,

391
00:48:08,420 --> 00:48:17,000
making us more susceptible to positive emotions and more resilient in the face of painful emotions.

392
00:48:17,680 --> 00:48:30,500
How many people know that three times a week, physical exercise, 30 minutes each time, has the same effect as our most powerful psychiatric drugs?

393
00:48:32,680 --> 00:48:34,720
Three times a week for 30 minutes.

394
00:48:35,880 --> 00:48:41,940
How many psychologists or psychiatrists prescribe, run three times a week and come see me in the morning?

395
00:48:43,800 --> 00:48:44,820
Not enough.

396
00:48:45,340 --> 00:48:53,380
That's practical idealism. Couple together. How about in conflict resolution?

397
00:48:53,760 --> 00:49:05,160
The dominant theme of most people with good intentions who want to resolve conflict is let's get the people together, let's get them to talk, and they and we will live happily ever after.

398
00:49:05,760 --> 00:49:12,900
Well, we have research from 1954, those of you who have taken social psych,

399
00:49:13,420 --> 00:49:22,360
Muzaffar Sharif, showing that the contact hypothesis, which is just get people to talk to one another, doesn't work.

400
00:49:22,360 --> 00:49:33,660
In fact, very often it worsens the situation. Very often the conflict actually gets worse as a result of just getting together and talking.

401
00:49:33,960 --> 00:49:35,720
That contact is not enough.

402
00:49:37,620 --> 00:49:44,060
That what you need, in the words of Muzaffar Sharif and later elaborated on by Elliot Aronson,

403
00:49:44,640 --> 00:49:56,320
what you need is a superordinate goal. A goal that you have to carry out together that you cannot do by yourself. Carry out together with a conflicting group. And that's how over time

404
00:49:56,320 --> 00:50:00,120
you resolve conflicts. Not just bring people together.

405
00:50:00,480 --> 00:50:03,320
Now, as you can imagine, this is very close to home for me.

406
00:50:04,680 --> 00:50:06,320
Because there were many people

407
00:50:07,480 --> 00:50:08,760
on both sides

408
00:50:09,460 --> 00:50:12,160
of the Arab-Israeli conflict who wanted to end it.

409
00:50:12,760 --> 00:50:14,840
Many people in this country

410
00:50:15,560 --> 00:50:22,900
who wanted to end it. So what did they do? Let's just get them together. Let's get them in a room, whether it was in Camp David or whether it was in Oslo

411
00:50:23,760 --> 00:50:26,100
or in Egypt. Let's just get them together

412
00:50:26,620 --> 00:50:31,840
to talk, to resolve their conflict and the issue, and then we will all live happily ever after.

413
00:50:32,380 --> 00:50:33,140
What happened?

414
00:50:34,640 --> 00:50:35,980
The situation worsened.

415
00:50:38,490 --> 00:50:42,540
Now, we have the data. We have known that.

416
00:50:43,740 --> 00:50:53,750
Muzaffar Sharif showed that in 1954 that that is the most likelihood outcome of just contact hypothesis, of just getting them to be together.

417
00:50:54,450 --> 00:51:01,950
And there were many people trying to resolve the conflict, not just in the Middle East, elsewhere in the world, with very good intentions,

418
00:51:02,790 --> 00:51:10,230
but very often making inadvertently the matters worse. Idealism and good intentions are not enough.

419
00:51:11,890 --> 00:51:16,450
We need to merge, to mate the research with the practice.

420
00:51:19,520 --> 00:51:21,080
and this is where you come in.

421
00:51:23,130 --> 00:51:31,990
This is where you come in, taking it seriously. Now, when I say take it seriously, there is a problem here because sometimes research doesn't deliver good news.

422
00:51:32,210 --> 00:51:42,350
It would be much easier and nicer if we could just simply get Israelis and Arabs together and the conflict would end. It would be much nicer, easier, smoother.

423
00:51:43,970 --> 00:51:55,590
It would be much easier if we could just cultivate children's self-esteem by giving them positive feedback, telling them how wonderful they are. It's easy to do, right? It feels good. They feel good. We feel good.

424
00:51:55,790 --> 00:51:59,230
But in the long run, it doesn't help if it's only that.

425
00:52:00,230 --> 00:52:01,250
Much easier.

426
00:52:01,950 --> 00:52:08,590
And research very often delivers bad news, saying it's not enough, the contact. It's not enough to praise.

427
00:52:10,680 --> 00:52:18,120
And then people choose, subconsciously, not consciously, subconsciously to ignore the research and go with their heart.

428
00:52:18,120 --> 00:52:25,120
And that's important to go with the heart, but it's important to go with the heart and the mind.

429
00:52:27,060 --> 00:52:32,300
Imagine if an aeronautics engineer woke up in the morning and said,

430
00:52:34,660 --> 00:52:39,620
you know, the law of gravity thing really makes things difficult for me.

431
00:52:40,040 --> 00:52:41,040
It's a pain.

432
00:52:41,520 --> 00:52:44,120
Things would be so much easier without the law of gravity.

433
00:52:44,520 --> 00:52:50,420
The design would be simpler. and he designs an airplane that ignores the law of gravity.

434
00:52:51,240 --> 00:52:55,220
What kind of airplane machine would he or she design?

435
00:52:55,960 --> 00:53:01,220
A failure. An aeronautics engineer takes into consideration reality.

436
00:53:01,620 --> 00:53:06,460
In reality, there is the law of gravity. We deal with that.

437
00:53:07,300 --> 00:53:13,220
Similarly, what research shows us is reality. What's out there, what's working and what's not working.

438
00:53:14,040 --> 00:53:24,620
And we need to conform to it. Take it into consideration, and it's up to you to take responsibility for that,

439
00:53:24,780 --> 00:53:32,840
to bridge the ivory tower in Main Street. You who are being exposed in 32 classes throughout your Harvard career

440
00:53:32,840 --> 00:53:41,620
to the most rigorous thinking on different topics have to take it and apply it, whether it's in psychology, whether it's in economics,

441
00:53:41,960 --> 00:53:45,040
obviously in engineering or computer science,

442
00:53:46,560 --> 00:53:50,300
where it's done much more readily than in the social sciences and the humanities.

443
00:53:51,840 --> 00:53:56,920
It's important to take responsibility because no one else is going to do it for you. No one is coming.

444
00:54:03,000 --> 00:54:17,450
Premise two, to be a practical idealist,

445
00:54:18,730 --> 00:54:28,070
the foundation of it has to be the belief that change is possible. Because if change was not possible on the individual level, on the societal level, why am I doing what I'm doing?

446
00:54:29,170 --> 00:54:33,670
Why would I spend time? Let me just be a hedonist and try and enjoy my life as much as possible.

447
00:54:35,590 --> 00:54:41,550
Now, for many of you, when you look at this, you may say, well, okay, yeah, change is possible. I believe that.

448
00:54:41,550 --> 00:54:53,410
And why do we need to have it as a basic premise of the course as opposed to the change is elusive? Well, in the context of a psychology course, it's anything but trivial.

449
00:54:53,810 --> 00:54:55,250
The change is possible.

450
00:54:56,250 --> 00:55:00,290
Let me share with you one study to illustrate what I mean.

451
00:55:04,320 --> 00:55:13,980
The Minnesota Twin Study, one of the most famous studies in the field of psychology, was done by Lykin and Talagan, two prominent psychologists.

452
00:55:15,400 --> 00:55:21,380
And what they did was they wanted to understand how much do genes matter?

453
00:55:23,700 --> 00:55:27,480
How much is it about nature versus nurture?

454
00:55:27,840 --> 00:55:29,360
So how do you test that?

455
00:55:29,780 --> 00:55:34,180
You look at identical twins, monozygotic twins.

456
00:55:36,600 --> 00:55:41,480
Who share the same genetic profile.

457
00:55:41,980 --> 00:55:46,980
And you look at those who are reared apart.

458
00:55:47,840 --> 00:55:56,780
Because if they're reared with the same parents, one could argue, well, they came out very similar because same environment, they look the same, they go to the same schools, same parents, and so on.

459
00:55:56,920 --> 00:56:05,800
But what if you're able to find identical twins who were separated at birth and reared in radically different environments?

460
00:56:06,540 --> 00:56:12,340
Well, you could. And they found a significant number of those reared sometimes on different continents.

461
00:56:15,340 --> 00:56:16,700
And they studied them.

462
00:56:17,080 --> 00:56:19,820
And what they found, remarkably,

463
00:56:21,940 --> 00:56:28,480
was significant similarities among these twins, sometimes to the point of the unbelievable.

464
00:56:29,220 --> 00:56:32,760
Like one set of twins, I think the name was,

465
00:56:33,600 --> 00:56:40,960
found a wife with the same name. They were reared different countries, didn't know about each other until the age of 37,

466
00:56:41,820 --> 00:56:43,320
married similar wives,

467
00:56:44,180 --> 00:56:45,880
enjoyed drinking the same beer,

468
00:56:47,460 --> 00:56:53,980
called their children by the same names. There were some mind-boggling similarities.

469
00:56:54,340 --> 00:56:57,320
This was an exception, but there were quite a few of those exceptions.

470
00:56:58,040 --> 00:57:04,940
But more interesting for psychologists was that their personality was incredibly similar.

471
00:57:06,880 --> 00:57:09,780
And very interesting for positive psychologists,

472
00:57:10,420 --> 00:57:12,980
those concerned with well-being and happiness,

473
00:57:13,720 --> 00:57:19,200
their well-being and happiness levels were incredibly similar,

474
00:57:20,830 --> 00:57:25,130
even if they were raised in radically different environments.

475
00:57:29,520 --> 00:57:38,500
Lycan and Talagan published a paper, a very influential paper in the 80s, which they called happiness as a stochastic phenomenon.

476
00:57:39,100 --> 00:57:44,500
Happiness as a stochastic phenomenon. And they end this paper with the following quote.

477
00:57:45,620 --> 00:57:54,020
It may be that trying to be happier is as futile as trying to be taller and is therefore counterproductive.

478
00:57:55,500 --> 00:57:58,120
This quote made me very unhappy on two accounts.

479
00:58:02,060 --> 00:58:06,840
This was a very influential quote. I mean, this appeared in the New York Times. They talked about it on television.

480
00:58:06,840 --> 00:58:18,290
that were interviewed, very problematic, because what are we doing here? If this is the outcome of research and rigorous research that they did,

481
00:58:18,390 --> 00:58:26,010
I mean, not easy research to go and look around the world, what can we do about it? Well, here is my response to that.

482
00:58:27,870 --> 00:58:32,530
Very simply, change is possible. And again, don't take my word for it, of course.

483
00:58:32,930 --> 00:58:40,910
Let me discuss that further, elaborate. There is counter-evidence where people actually do change.

484
00:58:41,070 --> 00:58:46,070
And we have research showing that people go into therapy, very often change as a result of therapy.

485
00:58:47,650 --> 00:58:57,270
Work by Albert Bendura, Stanford psychologist, shows that very often people encountering one special sentence,

486
00:58:57,850 --> 00:59:01,590
reading it or hearing it from something, that sentence can change their lives.

487
00:59:02,790 --> 00:59:11,110
reading a certain book, having certain experiences, positive or painful experiences.

488
00:59:11,110 --> 00:59:19,410
There is this concept of post-traumatic stress disorder, PTSD. There is also the concept of post-traumatic growth.

489
00:59:21,510 --> 00:59:26,330
So people change up or down in their levels of happiness as a result of experiences.

490
00:59:27,370 --> 00:59:35,750
So there is counter-evidence that shows that not everyone's level of well-being is determined by their genes.

491
00:59:37,390 --> 00:59:48,490
In fact, there is research that shows that, yes, genes do matter, and they matter a lot. But as we'll talk about during the week on change, other things matter as well.

492
00:59:48,490 --> 01:00:00,680
And the error that Lycan and Talagan and many others make when they generalize and say change is not possible is what I call the error of the average.

493
01:00:00,920 --> 01:00:09,840
Yes, on average, when you looked at this group of 40 or 50 twins reared apart, when you look at the average, they're just about the same.

494
01:00:10,860 --> 01:00:17,320
However, that's not looking at individuals because while many of them are the same, not all are the same.

495
01:00:17,320 --> 01:00:28,080
You know, it reminds me of a joke about the statistician who drowned in a pool with an average height of 10 inches.

496
01:00:31,120 --> 01:00:34,300
Drowned in a pool with an average height of 10 inches.

497
01:00:34,720 --> 01:00:39,540
You see, you cannot tell the height or the depth, rather, of a pool based on the average.

498
01:00:40,200 --> 01:00:46,720
Because that pool may be average of 10 inches, but it may have places that are 20 feet deep, if it's a large pool.

499
01:00:47,480 --> 01:00:51,580
The same when you look at the average of individuals and twins.

500
01:00:52,920 --> 01:01:03,560
The majority, or on average, they're extremely similar, but they're also outliers. And very often it's the outliers, the differences, that are the most interesting,

501
01:01:03,600 --> 01:01:12,640
because they stretch not just our imagination, they stretch our ability to understand when and where change is possible.

502
01:01:13,520 --> 01:01:20,560
The question when we see exceptions, whether it's the Lycan intelligence study, where we do see exceptions, not all twins were the same.

503
01:01:22,020 --> 01:01:23,500
Majority were, but not all.

504
01:01:24,700 --> 01:01:35,340
The question is no longer whether or not change is possible, but rather how is change possible. Once again, the exception proves the rule.

505
01:01:35,880 --> 01:01:40,760
And the research that argues that it's not possible to change is detrimental.

506
01:01:41,320 --> 01:01:57,700
Think about an eight-year-old girl who's very unhappy and then reads in some magazine about that study saying that basically your genetic set point, what you're born with, is there for life.

507
01:01:58,740 --> 01:02:07,980
And she's unhappy and she feels anxious and miserable as an eight-year-old and she says to herself, that's it, that's my lot in life.

508
01:02:08,420 --> 01:02:09,500
I was born unlucky.

509
01:02:11,400 --> 01:02:14,260
And that very often becomes a self-fulfilling prophecy.

510
01:02:14,760 --> 01:02:21,740
And she remains unhappy. And sometimes it even makes her less happy than she was before. Because now she's also helpless.

511
01:02:23,320 --> 01:02:28,620
Change is possible. You know, I often say that I'm the right person to teach positive psychology.

512
01:02:29,420 --> 01:02:34,920
Why? Because I wasn't born with quote-unquote happy genes.

513
01:02:35,700 --> 01:02:45,400
I was born with, genetically speaking, with relatively high levels of anxiety, inclination toward rumination, over-examination.

514
01:02:45,840 --> 01:02:47,820
We'll talk about that later in the course.

515
01:02:50,290 --> 01:02:57,910
I went into positive psychology, into the field of psychology, as I mentioned in the first lecture, because I was unhappy here.

516
01:02:59,010 --> 01:03:09,830
And over time, as a result of many of these studies, a result of examination, a result of asking also the right questions, I have become happier.

517
01:03:10,670 --> 01:03:13,550
So on a personal level, I know it's possible to become happier.

518
01:03:14,190 --> 01:03:20,610
Now, I'm happier today than I was 15 years ago when I started. I hope to be happier 15 years today from today than I am today.

519
01:03:21,690 --> 01:03:26,950
It's a lifelong process, but it is possible, and many people show that it is possible.

520
01:03:26,950 --> 01:03:36,270
And those who argue that it's not possible and use science to argue for that very often are hurting more than helping the field.

521
01:03:37,690 --> 01:03:47,030
Now, by the way, Lycan and Talagan were interviewed recently in a Times Magazine issue on happiness.

522
01:03:47,960 --> 01:03:52,690
And let me quote to you what they said.

523
01:03:54,490 --> 01:03:58,850
This is in 2005, Lycan. I made a dumb statement.

524
01:03:59,290 --> 01:04:03,790
It is clear that we can change happiness levels, up or down.

525
01:04:04,610 --> 01:04:08,190
So they went back on their statement. You know, it was a sensationalist statement at the time.

526
01:04:09,390 --> 01:04:10,990
It certainly is possible.

527
01:04:13,620 --> 01:04:16,680
So how do we do research that's more responsible,

528
01:04:17,280 --> 01:04:20,180
that does not lead to detrimental results,

529
01:04:21,100 --> 01:04:23,200
and at the same time, true?

530
01:04:23,920 --> 01:04:29,840
You know, we don't want to invent research. Research is about identifying things that really occur, happen in reality.

531
01:04:29,840 --> 01:04:38,340
So one of the first things that I will argue for in terms of healthy research is let's also focus on what is working.

532
01:04:38,560 --> 01:04:42,120
That's the first thing that we discussed in the past lecture.

533
01:04:42,620 --> 01:04:49,160
The second thing that I will argue for in addition to studying what works is also to study the best.

534
01:04:51,530 --> 01:05:01,850
What do I mean by that? So it's not just let's study what makes people happy. Let's not just study happy people. Let's not just study what makes happy relationships and good relationships.

535
01:05:02,130 --> 01:05:04,350
Let's study the happiest people.

536
01:05:04,990 --> 01:05:11,730
Let's study the most successful relationships and learn from that.

537
01:05:13,190 --> 01:05:24,530
Now, that is a radically different approach than studying just the average. Because what I'm saying here is let's not study the average. Let's study the top 5% so that we can understand a phenomenon better.

538
01:05:24,530 --> 01:05:32,270
The person who talks about this is Abraham Maslow when he talks about growing tip statistics.

539
01:05:32,950 --> 01:05:38,340
Let me read to you. This is taken from his book.

540
01:05:41,860 --> 01:05:49,600
What this kind of research design means is a change in our conception of statistics and especially of sampling theory.

541
01:05:49,920 --> 01:05:59,340
What I'm frankly espousing here is what I've been calling growing tip statistics, taking my title from the fact that it is at the growing tip of a plant

542
01:05:59,340 --> 01:06:03,480
that the greatest genetic action takes place.

543
01:06:04,460 --> 01:06:10,000
What he's saying is that let's study the sages, the saints, the extraordinary people,

544
01:06:10,760 --> 01:06:19,860
the arrowhead, so that we can understand and realize the potential in all people.

545
01:06:22,080 --> 01:06:26,380
Let me quote him in greater length. This is very important, which is why I'm doing it.

546
01:06:28,300 --> 01:06:36,260
If we want to know how fast a human being can run, then it is no use to average out the speed of a good sample of the population.

547
01:06:36,500 --> 01:06:47,240
It is far better to collect Olympic gold medal winners and see how well they can do. If we want to know the possibilities for spiritual growth, value growth, or moral development in human beings,

548
01:06:47,440 --> 01:06:53,060
then I maintain that we can learn most by studying our most moral, ethical, and saintly people.

549
01:06:53,060 --> 01:07:01,200
On the whole, I think it is fair to say that human history is a record of the ways in which human nature has been sold short.

550
01:07:01,780 --> 01:07:07,580
The highest possibility of human nature has practically always been underrated.

551
01:07:08,180 --> 01:07:19,640
Certainly it seems more and more clear that what we call normal in psychology is really a psychopathology of the average. so undramatic and so widely spread

552
01:07:19,640 --> 01:07:23,120
that we don't even notice it ordinarily.

553
01:07:27,470 --> 01:07:32,070
Do you see the implications of what he's saying here?

554
01:07:33,030 --> 01:07:34,430
Essentially what he's saying is

555
01:07:36,070 --> 01:07:45,510
let's not just study why do most individuals fail. Let's also study why do some, not many, but why do some individuals succeed despite the circumstances.

556
01:07:46,150 --> 01:07:51,850
Let's not study just the average that says that people can't really change.

557
01:07:52,610 --> 01:07:59,590
Let's study those people who have changed, who have literally transformed their lives and the lives of those around them.

558
01:08:00,790 --> 01:08:07,990
This is a radical approach to research. This is a radical approach to the search,

559
01:08:08,690 --> 01:08:11,030
to studying ourselves as well.

560
01:08:12,090 --> 01:08:23,130
Because very often if we only study the average, we only see the average, we only see the geometrical shapes and completely miss the children on the bus. And very often, the answer to some of our most pressing questions

561
01:08:23,130 --> 01:08:25,630
lies in the extraordinary.

562
01:08:27,290 --> 01:08:28,870
Lies in the children on the bus.

563
01:08:32,060 --> 01:08:37,880
Now, does anyone other than me, because I admit I fall into this category,

564
01:08:38,700 --> 01:08:43,260
but anyone other than me feel a little bit of dis-ease when I talk about this?

565
01:08:45,970 --> 01:08:48,350
Seriously, a little bit of dis-ease when I talk about

566
01:08:48,990 --> 01:08:59,030
let's focus on and study the best, the saints, the sages, the extraordinary, I feel dis-ease. I'm sure some of you feel that too.

567
01:08:59,810 --> 01:09:05,610
Because after all, isn't that elitist?

568
01:09:12,250 --> 01:09:16,210
Shouldn't we study the average because we're concerned not just with the elites,

569
01:09:16,790 --> 01:09:20,630
we're concerned with the average? So there are two answers to that.

570
01:09:22,510 --> 01:09:29,730
Why I still maintain, and I must admit, I still feel some dis-ease every time I teach this, every time I think about this.

571
01:09:30,990 --> 01:09:39,070
And then I think to why it is so important to study the best, why the growing tip statistics is such an important approach to research

572
01:09:39,070 --> 01:09:43,210
that I encourage my students to carry out.

573
01:09:43,790 --> 01:09:48,830
First of all, because it's not to the exclusion of the average.

574
01:09:49,210 --> 01:09:57,790
Just like positive psychology does not say, let's exclude what's not working, let's exclude study of pathology.

575
01:09:58,450 --> 01:10:07,250
Similarly, growing tip statistic is not saying let's not study the average. It says let's also study the best.

576
01:10:07,590 --> 01:10:17,510
So this is the first thing to alleviate the concern of elitism. But the second issue, the second response is even more important.

577
01:10:18,930 --> 01:10:26,470
Because everyone, everyone benefits when we study the best. and the quote-unquote average, whatever that means,

578
01:10:27,930 --> 01:10:34,110
the quote-unquote average benefits even more than the best from this kind of studies. Why?

579
01:10:34,670 --> 01:10:37,270
For example, the study of resilience.

580
01:10:38,150 --> 01:10:46,210
We could have gone on and studied the average at-risk population for decades and centuries

581
01:10:46,210 --> 01:10:51,310
and very little advances would have been made, very little advances were made.

582
01:10:51,310 --> 01:10:59,690
It was only when we started to study those, quote-unquote, best examples,

583
01:11:00,530 --> 01:11:05,950
those successful kids, those, quote-unquote, super kids, as they were initially named.

584
01:11:06,330 --> 01:11:14,050
It's only when we started to study those that we actually understood how we can best help that population.

585
01:11:14,730 --> 01:11:21,090
And once we applied what we studied, the resilience issue, everyone benefited and continues to benefit from it.

586
01:11:21,930 --> 01:11:24,890
That's an example of growing tip statistic research.

587
01:11:25,750 --> 01:11:29,430
Or how about the study of meditation? So I want to study how to meditate.

588
01:11:30,030 --> 01:11:35,370
Do I go out to Harvard Yard and take a random sample of sophomores to study meditation?

589
01:11:35,970 --> 01:11:42,110
Or do I go to a mountaintop in Tibet and study the people who have been doing it for decades?

590
01:11:42,750 --> 01:11:46,310
Of course I go and study them. And this is exactly what psychologists did.

591
01:11:46,670 --> 01:11:50,610
And they studied their brain. And we'll talk about it when we talk about meditation.

592
01:11:51,310 --> 01:11:54,710
And they illustrated how the brain is transformed through meditation.

593
01:11:56,070 --> 01:12:00,150
And psychologists like John Kabat-Zinn and Richard Davidson and Herbert Benson

594
01:12:00,150 --> 01:12:08,490
were able to take what they learned from these extraordinary, best individuals

595
01:12:08,490 --> 01:12:13,570
and apply it to other people's lives so that now I benefit from meditation.

596
01:12:15,170 --> 01:12:26,470
When I do it for 15 or 20 minutes a day and millions of other people, The average benefits a great deal because of the study of the best, of the growing tip.

597
01:12:28,520 --> 01:12:29,740
How about relationships?

598
01:12:30,140 --> 01:12:36,980
Can you imagine a study of relationships throughout human history that focuses on the average?

599
01:12:37,940 --> 01:12:40,820
What's the average relationship in human history?

600
01:12:41,360 --> 01:12:46,300
The average relationship in human history is one in which the woman is subjugated.

601
01:12:47,340 --> 01:12:49,960
That's the average relationship in human history.

602
01:12:52,390 --> 01:12:55,170
Now, what if we just studied that? Would that be helpful?

603
01:12:56,710 --> 01:12:59,310
No, it was when people like John Stuart Mill,

604
01:13:00,170 --> 01:13:04,070
who studied his relationship, which was at the time extraordinary,

605
01:13:05,790 --> 01:13:12,090
realized the potential of what all relationships can be, that he wrote his book on the subjugation of women.

606
01:13:12,950 --> 01:13:23,390
One of the most important books of the 19th century that has led to the feminist movement and to the equality movement.

607
01:13:23,390 --> 01:13:26,550
But what if we only studied the average? Would that have helped relationships?

608
01:13:27,010 --> 01:13:27,850
Not at all.

609
01:13:28,690 --> 01:13:29,850
How about teaching?

610
01:13:32,280 --> 01:13:43,420
What do you want to do in order to learn about teaching? Go and study the average teachers or go and study Marva Collins and then apply what Marva Collins does to all teachers.

611
01:13:45,580 --> 01:13:56,940
Everyone benefits when we focus on the tip of the stem. And this is why Maslow said that human nature and human potential has been sold short when we only study the average.

612
01:14:00,450 --> 01:14:09,610
It's also about studying our personal best, not just other people's best, whether it's our personal best experiences.

613
01:14:09,610 --> 01:14:16,070
Because if we study our best experiences, when we were at our happiest, when we were at our most successful,

614
01:14:16,070 --> 01:14:22,890
When we thrive the most within the relationship, we can learn from it and apply it to our future as well.

615
01:14:25,390 --> 01:14:34,470
When we study our average, we're describing our lives. When we're studying the best within ourselves, we're potentially prescribing.

616
01:14:37,550 --> 01:14:48,330
Maslow again, few in number though they may be, we can learn a great deal about values from the direct study of these highly evolved, most mature, psychologically healthiest individuals.

617
01:14:48,330 --> 01:14:56,610
and from the study of the peak moments of average individuals, moments in which they became transiently self-actualized.

618
01:14:57,630 --> 01:15:09,430
If we learn from these experiences in ourselves, the question is no longer whether or not it's possible to experience it more and more in our lives. The question is, how is it possible to experience it?

619
01:15:16,280 --> 01:15:26,720
All right. So we talked about changing ourselves, how it's possible, the growing tip statistic, which is the second significant idea. within research in positive psychology.

620
01:15:26,880 --> 01:15:29,620
The first significant idea is let's study what works.

621
01:15:31,020 --> 01:15:35,000
The second significant idea is let's study what works best.

622
01:15:35,860 --> 01:15:44,140
But this is about individual change. How about societal change? I want to share with you an end today with a study.

623
01:15:45,080 --> 01:15:49,980
One of the most famous studies in the field called the Cambridge-Somerville Youth Study.

624
01:15:51,180 --> 01:16:07,520
This was a study that was run starting in the 1930s right here, between Harvard, MIT, where the best minds, psychological minds, philosophical minds, psychiatrists, got together and said, let's create the Rolls-Royce.

625
01:16:07,720 --> 01:16:12,960
I don't even know if there was a Rolls-Royce then, but the best intervention program that we can think of.

626
01:16:13,360 --> 01:16:16,800
There was no limit in terms of how much money was put into it.

627
01:16:17,480 --> 01:16:23,300
As much as they needed, they got. And they chose 250 kids from an at-risk population.

628
01:16:24,400 --> 01:16:32,760
And the intervention was not a quick fix, overnight change, weekend seminar, five-year intervention. And here is what they got.

629
01:16:33,100 --> 01:16:40,620
Twice a month, caseworkers visited them, helped them deal with conflicts in the family, helped them deal with issues in their lives.

630
01:16:42,560 --> 01:16:47,460
Half of them had academic tutoring, those who needed it, got help.

631
01:16:48,120 --> 01:16:48,860
Academic help.

632
01:16:49,780 --> 01:16:58,180
Psychiatric attention, all those who needed it, they were there. No limits on how much you needed. Whatever you needed, you got from the best minds in the field.

633
01:16:59,480 --> 01:17:04,100
They joined the Boy Scouts, YMCA, other youth movements,

634
01:17:04,500 --> 01:17:09,060
benefited a great deal, supposedly, from these experiences. They got everything.

635
01:17:09,420 --> 01:17:16,620
This would be a dream treatment, not just in the 1930s, today as well. This is what psychologists dream about at night.

636
01:17:17,460 --> 01:17:19,040
Just to introduce this.

637
01:17:22,090 --> 01:17:25,650
And then measuring outcomes was as serious as the program itself.

638
01:17:26,030 --> 01:17:31,030
There was a random assignment. There were 250 kids who got nothing, who were also studied,

639
01:17:31,450 --> 01:17:37,590
just like the kids who got the five-year intervention were studied. 250 kids in the control group.

640
01:17:38,650 --> 01:17:47,250
40 years follow-up. So this was not just about change today, tomorrow for the five years. They followed them through much of their lives.

641
01:17:47,910 --> 01:17:51,170
This was a serious study. This was a serious intervention.

642
01:17:52,630 --> 01:17:55,150
And the results were shocking.

643
01:17:56,910 --> 01:18:05,330
Even though all those who participated in the study, whether it was the mental health workers, whether it was the philosophers and the psychologists and the professors

644
01:18:05,330 --> 01:18:16,070
and the psychiatrists, praised the program as the best, as highly effective, when they looked at the raw objective data, the results were shocking.

645
01:18:17,410 --> 01:18:22,990
Juvenile offenses, control group versus intervention group, no difference.

646
01:18:23,930 --> 01:18:34,670
Over a third had official records and 20 more percent had unofficial records for misdemeanors. No difference in juvenile offenses.

647
01:18:35,530 --> 01:18:40,690
Adult offenses later on in their lives, again, no difference.

648
01:18:40,690 --> 01:18:48,890
over 20% offenses, whether against property or against person in both groups, the 250 here versus

649
01:18:48,890 --> 01:18:57,050
the 250 here, which is a significant sample size, no difference whatsoever. Other measures, physical

650
01:18:57,050 --> 01:19:04,190
health and mental health, no difference whatsoever. But finally, there was a significant

651
01:19:04,190 --> 01:19:11,270
difference on alcoholism, the number of people who became alcoholic later on in life, as well as

652
01:19:11,270 --> 01:19:16,610
job status, how many people were able to get into what they termed the white-collar jobs.

653
01:19:17,430 --> 01:19:19,910
So at least there are results there.

654
01:19:20,830 --> 01:19:26,650
At least they found statistically significant results when it came to that.

655
01:19:27,270 --> 01:19:28,310
That's good, right?

656
01:19:33,230 --> 01:19:45,200
Not at all. Because these results were, quote-unquote, in the wrong direction, meaning there were more alcoholists in the intervention group than in the control group.

657
01:19:45,820 --> 01:19:50,320
There were more people in the control group making it, quote-unquote, at work,

658
01:19:51,960 --> 01:19:55,920
raising their status at work, than in the intervention group.

659
01:19:57,560 --> 01:20:00,260
In other words, the intervention did more harm than good.

660
01:20:01,580 --> 01:20:04,800
Idealism, good intention, a lot of money, wasn't practical.

661
01:20:05,880 --> 01:20:16,320
Now, many people who look at this study, this is a seminal study, there are very few studies in the history of psychology that were that serious. They say, well, societal change is probably not possible.

662
01:20:17,300 --> 01:20:19,540
Give me one minute and I'll finish.

663
01:20:20,060 --> 01:20:22,140
Societal change is not possible, they say.

664
01:20:23,180 --> 01:20:29,880
Is it? Well, first of all, there are exceptions, and when we have exceptions, that proves the rule.

665
01:20:29,980 --> 01:20:37,640
There are programs that actually work, whether it's the work, again, of Karen Ravich and Marty Seligman from UPenn Resilience Program,

666
01:20:37,640 --> 01:20:48,760
Whether it's Marva Collins, who is certainly an exception, who shows how interventions work. And it's interesting to think about the difference in what Marva Collins does, where she doesn't give the students a sense of entitlement,

667
01:20:49,080 --> 01:20:54,020
where she praises them but she gives them hard love as opposed to a free lunch,

668
01:20:54,980 --> 01:21:04,020
where she doesn't label them as needy, and the study perhaps labeled these kids as needy. There are many differences, but the key is to study this exception

669
01:21:04,020 --> 01:21:13,940
and for practical idealists to come together and to say, what is working? Let's study the best. Let's study what works and then apply it.

670
01:21:15,220 --> 01:21:16,620
Let's spread the word.

671
01:21:17,380 --> 01:21:25,400
Let's do what Maslow talked about back in the 1950s. What he suggested is a Manhattan Project-type attack

672
01:21:25,400 --> 01:21:35,280
upon what I consider to be the truly big problem of our time, not only for psychology but for all human beings with any sense of historical urgency.

673
01:21:36,200 --> 01:21:42,800
The Manhattan Project, when they created the atomic bomb, and whether or not you agree with the Manhattan Project normatively,

674
01:21:43,540 --> 01:21:46,080
positively what... One second, I'm almost done.

675
01:21:46,900 --> 01:21:48,380
Positively, what they did there

676
01:21:48,920 --> 01:21:59,540
was bring together the best minds, Oppenheimer, Szilard, Fermi, Feynman, Bohr, bring them together with a mission of saving the free world.

677
01:21:59,760 --> 01:22:08,960
And again, whether you agree or not with the project is beside the point. It was the greatest, positively speaking, scientific project in history where their minds got together.

678
01:22:09,100 --> 01:22:13,300
And this is what Maslow is suggesting that psychologists do.

679
01:22:13,940 --> 01:22:21,240
This is also the aim of positive psychology, no less than this, to get people around the world to think about these problems, these issues,

680
01:22:21,600 --> 01:22:25,660
practical idealists who will study what works, who will study the best,

681
01:22:26,280 --> 01:22:28,540
and through that, make a difference.

682
01:22:29,240 --> 01:22:30,380
I'll see you next week.
